The earlier runs failed because the injected selector was never used. `SelectorFactory` keys an injected selector by its `getSObjectType()`, and the ApexMocks stub returned nothing for that call. The service then fell through to the real `OpportunitiesSelector`, which ran SOQL, found no rows, and so never called `registerNew`.

Stubbing `getSObjectType()` on the `IOpportunitiesSelector` mock fails to compile, so the test now wraps the ApexMocks stub in a thin test-only selector. The wrapper reports `Opportunity.SObjectType` so the factory registers it, and forwards `selectById` to the ApexMocks stub, which still supplies the data. The rest of the tests are unchanged.

