The earlier explanation was wrong. ApexMocks matches arguments by value, so passing a different `Set<Id>` instance was not the cause.

The real cause is that the selector mock never reported its SObject type. `Application.Selector.setMock` registers the mock under `getSObjectType()`, which returned null on the unstubbed mock. `newInstance(Opportunity.SObjectType)` therefore missed the mock and built the real `OpportunitiesSelector`. That ran SOQL against the empty test database and returned no rows, which is why every stale case got 0. The fix is to stub `getSObjectType()` to return `Opportunity.SObjectType` before `setMock`. The test now runs without SOQL, as the task requires.

